  §31.3.4 body_range (8728, 8790)  section_span (8728, 8790)
  --- §31.3.4 L8728-L8789 ---
   8728 ### 31.3.4 E(X) on a specifiable slice, and 540 predictions
   8729  The 30,108 pairs are a gzipped file this session could not read. But one slice of the catalogue is
   8730  specified exactly in print, and a slice is enough for the operator.
   8731 
   8732  Candelas, de la Ossa, He & Szendroi (Triadophilia, ATMP 12 (2008) 429) state that the KS list's χ = ±6
   8733  points have Hodge numbers (h, h+3) and (h+3, h) for 13 ≤ h ≤ 128, and name the exclusions: h = 102,
   8734  103, 115, 117 and 119–126. That is a complete cell set — 208 cells — and ℛ applies to it.
   8735 
   8736     E(X) = 540. 498 join failures and 498 meet failures of 21,528 pairs.
   8737 
   8738  24.3.4.1 Which is 540 predictions, not a defect
   8739  Chapter 6 reads E(X) > 0 as what a structure admits and lacks. The periodic table admits 36 such
   8740  cells and they are the elements. Here the slice admits 540 and they are Hodge pairs the
   8741  catalogue must contain if the pair set is closed.
   8742 
   8743  The slice cannot contain them, and the reason is geometric. Two parallel lines have no fixed point:
   8744  the join of (h, h+3) and (h+3, h) is (h+3, h+3). So ℛ immediately names the diagonal.
   8745 
   8746   the predictions
   8747   cells                                     540
   8748   distinct χ values                         5 — only 0, ±2, ±4
   8749   on the diagonal, χ = 0                    112, from (13,13) to (131,131)
   8750   h¹¹ + h²¹ range                           26 to 262
   8751 
   8752  The slice was cut at χ = ±6 and the closure predicts inward. It says the set the slice came from fills
   8753  the gap between the two lines — which is where the rest of the Kreuzer–Skarke list lives.
   8754 
   8755  24.3.4.2 The fourth falsification test, run
   8756 
   8757  The file is missing, so the predictions cannot be looked up. They can still be refuted, because
   8758  the literature states where the catalogue's contents lie.
   8759 
   8760   test                                                                     result
   8761   h¹¹ ≥ 1 and h²¹ ≥ 1                                                      540 / 540
   8762   h¹¹ + h²¹ ≤ 502, the published maximum                                   540 / 540
   8763   mirror partner present                                                   540 / 540
   8764   falling where Candelas et al. record "essentially every site occupied"   540 / 540
   8765   falling in the "thinly populated" tip, where a prediction would be       0 / 540
   8766   refuted
   8767   falsified                                                                0
   8768   individually named in print                                              X₁₉,₁₉ (split bicubic), X₂₀,₂₀ (the 24-cell)
   8769 
   8770     Zero falsifications, two named confirmations, and every prediction inside the region
   8771     published as fully populated.
   8772 
   8773  This is weaker than a lookup and it is not nothing. A prediction landing in the sparse tip would have
   8774  been refuted, and none does. The test is against the literature's characterisation of the file
   8775  rather than against the file.
   8776 
   8777  24.3.4.3 And the void reads the same way
   8778 
   8779                                                                                     cells        predictions
   8780   all h from 13 to 128                                                              232          589
   8781   with the documented exclusions                                                    208          540
   8782 
   8783  The 24 excluded values — 102, 103, 115, 117 and the run 119–126 — reduce the predictions by
   8784  49. The gaps in the published list are not noise with respect to closure: removing them moves the set
   8785  toward its own normal form, which is what §10 says a void should do when it is structural rather than
   8786  accidental.
   8787 
   8788  What remains of item D is the lookup. 540 named cells, a file, and an afternoon.
   8789 
